3. **Calculate the time to return:**  
   The distance to return is the same **30 miles**, but the speed is now **6 mph**. Using the formula:  
   $$
   \text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}}
   $$  
   Substituting the values:  
   $$
   \text{Time} = \frac{30 \, \text{miles}}{6 \, \text{mph}} = 5 \, \text{hours}
   $$

**Final Answer:**  
\boxed{5}  
**Reasoning:** Tom travels 30 miles away at 10 mph, then takes 5 hours to return at 6 mph. The key steps are calculating the one-way distance and applying the return speed to find the required time.  
